/**
 * @page topic_testing Testing with Catch2 v3
 *
 * ascii-chat uses Catch2 v3 for native unit, contract, integration, and
 * benchmark tests on Windows, Linux, and macOS. The authoritative build and
 * test targets are in `tests/CMakeLists.txt` and `CMakePresets.json`.
 *
 * @section testing_build Building and selecting tests
 * Configure the test preset, build it, and let CTest schedule discovered
 * cases. CTest is the parallel scheduler; Catch2 cases are not run in
 * parallel inside one process.
 * @code{.sh}
 * cmake --preset tests
 * cmake --build --preset tests
 * ctest --preset all --output-on-failure --parallel 0
 * ctest --preset unit
 * ctest --preset integration
 * ctest --preset performance
 * @endcode
 *
 * The performance CTest group remains for compatibility, but native timing
 * sources live in `tests/benchmarks/`. They use Catch2 `BENCHMARK` or
 * `BENCHMARK_ADVANCED`, are tagged `[benchmark]`, and run serially in CI.
 *
 * @section testing_writing Writing Catch2 tests
 * Native test files are C++ and use `TEST_CASE` with hierarchical tags.
 * @code{.cpp}
 * TEST_CASE("parser/rejects_truncated_header", "[parser][negative]") {
 *   SECTION("missing length") {
 *     REQUIRE(parse_header(bytes, 2) == ERROR_BUFFER_UNDERFLOW);
 *   }
 * }
 * @endcode
 *
 * Prefer `SECTION` for branches sharing setup, `REQUIRE` for fatal
 * preconditions, `CHECK` for collecting failures, and Catch2 matchers for
 * readable string/container assertions. Use `INFO` and `CAPTURE` for
 * diagnostics. Catch2 generators replace Catch2 theories:
 * @code{.cpp}
 * auto size = GENERATE(1u, 16u, 256u, 4096u);
 * CAPTURE(size);
 * REQUIRE(round_trip(size));
 * @endcode
 * Use `GENERATE_COPY` for mutable captured state. Keep generated values
 * deterministic and independent; never leave a declared data table unused.
 * Use RAII helpers or `TEST_CASE_METHOD` for fixtures, not uncalled setup or
 * teardown functions. Assertions stay on the Catch2 test thread: worker
 * callbacks record a result and the owning test asserts it after joining.
 *
 * @section testing_tags Tags and groups
 * Tags identify subsystem, capability, and cost, such as `[crypto]`,
 * `[network]`, `[integration]`, `[contract]`, `[slow]`,
 * `[requires-network]`, and `[benchmark]`. Select them with Catch2 expressions
 * such as `unit_tests "[crypto]~[slow]"`; use CTest labels for CI groups.
 *
 * @section testing_output Output, coverage, and CI
 * Catch2 discovery requests JUnit XML from every native Catch2 executable in
 * `build/Testing/catch2-xml/`. CTest also writes its own JUnit result file.
 * CI validates the Catch2 files, uploads them as artifacts, and passes the
 * same files to Codecov. Coverage builds instrument production targets and
 * Catch2 executables; the coverage workflow combines gcov/LLVM data before
 * uploading it. XML is diagnostic evidence and does not replace CTest's
 * pass/fail result.
 *
 * @section testing_standalone Standalone and external-boundary tests
 * New native assertions must not be standalone C programs. Convert them to
 * Catch2 and register them through CMake so they receive tags, discovery,
 * JUnit output, CTest parallelization, and coverage. A small executable may
 * remain only as an external-process fixture, PTY/console probe, or platform
 * boundary that Catch2 cannot own; `tests/standalone/README.md` documents
 * each exception. Python tests remain pytest tests and may launch subprocesses.
 *
 * @section testing_troubleshooting Troubleshooting
 * List tests with `unit_tests --list-tests` and inspect CTest with `ctest -N`.
 * Run a focused case with a Catch2 expression and write XML with
 * `--reporter junit --out path.xml`. Catch2 filters and XML directories
 * are no longer supported.
 */
